    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d123">

                <pb facs="0279" n="249" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v18/pg249"/>

                <head>123. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in Japan<anchor n="0" xml:id="d123-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1959-12-20T13:20:00-05:00">December 20, 1959,
                            1:20 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>1487. Embtel 1607,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d123-f2"/>Secto 31,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d123-f3"/>Embtel 1956.<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d123-f4"/> Following draft instructions referred to in Secto 31. Inform GOJ proposed minute (B) on Article III islands acceptable with
                    following changes: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>Rephrase opening clause of first sentence Japanese statement “While
                            the question of the status of the islands administered by the United
                            States under Article III of the Treaty of Peace with Japan has not been
                            made a subject of discussion . . . .”<anchor n="4" xml:id="d123-f5"/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Comment:</hi> Believe this language less awkward
                            than Japanese formulation.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>In Japanese statement delete “and the islanders are Japanese.” <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Comment:</hi> This expression not entirely
                            accurate since some residents <pb facs="0280" n="250" xml:id="pg250"/>
                            these islands not of Japanese ancestry or even Japanese nationals.
                            Furthermore this clause superfluous in light preceding clause expressing
                            Japanese concern re safety “people of these islands.”</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>Reword second clause US statement as follows: “intends to take the
                            necessary measures for the defense of these islands and to do . . .
                            etc.” <hi rendition="#italic">Comment:</hi> This language more closely
                            parallels Japanese statement and is less likely to be construed as US
                            commitment to Japan to defend islands.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d123-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d123-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 794.5/12–2059. Secret; Niact; Limit Distribution. Drafted by Sneider on December 10, cleared with L/FE in draft and by the Department of Defense as set
                    forth in Secto 31, and approved by
                        Parsons. Repeated to CINCPAC exclusive for POLAD and
                    Admiral Felt, COMUS/Japan exclusive for General Burns, and Naha exclusive for General
                        Booth and Blankenship.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d123-f2" n="1" xml:id="d123-f2-content">
                <p><ref target="#d106">Document 106</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d123-f3" n="2" xml:id="d123-f3-content">
                <p>Secto 31 from Paris, December 19, contained the view of Herter and Gates that they perferred to have no minute on or any reference
                    to Article III islands, but that if MacArthur believed that “such exchange virtually essential to
                    ratification prospect, then it was agreed that he could accept minute and United
                    States reply as amended in Department’s draft cable of instructions of December
                    10.” (Department of State, Central Files, 749C.0221/12–1959)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d123-f4" n="3" xml:id="d123-f4-content">
                <p>Telegram 1956 from Tokyo, December 20, urged the Department to send the
                    instructions mentioned in Secto 31. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ibid</hi>., 794.5/12–2059) See Supplement.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d123-f5" n="4" xml:id="d123-f5-content">
                <p>All ellipses are in the source text.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
